{{> judge-opening}}

JUDGMENT POINT: THE ARTICLE'S LANGUAGE (kogaki#1417): `language` in the input is the one language the article is written in, declared by the Audience file; write every proposition you compose in it, and keep in English only the keys and ids the record's shape fixes. compose ONE LEG for this Brief -- the original Leg whose paragraphs are `paragraphs` in the input, opening from what the Persona already knows, rendered in the input as `persona_prior_knowledge` (kogaki#1385, kogaki#1699). A Leg carries no reader state (kogaki#1699) and binds no Move (kogaki#1768): the paragraph Move is bound per Brief Waypoint by /paragraph-plan, after the Brief (kogaki#1375). The Harness marks the last Leg `reaches_target` (kogaki#1532). EVERY LEG CARRIES ITS WAYPOINTS (kogaki#1311): the ordered route through the Leg, ONE BRIEF WAYPOINT PER PARAGRAPH OF THE ORIGINAL LEG, IN ORDER (kogaki#1385), because a Brief Waypoint is one paragraph. {{> move-extraction-playground/each-waypoint-is-a-point}} Brief Waypoint N is the proposition paragraph N makes. THE WORDS ARE THE SEGMENT CONTENT'S (kogaki#1495): this Brief composes no renderings record, because each paragraph's Segment `content` clauses are already the article's own rendering of its source; Claim N's `proposition` and Brief Waypoint N's `point` are written in the words of paragraph N's Segment `content` clauses -- a word that is in none of them and is not a function word is refused naming the word and the Segments. Write the points from the paragraphs and their Extractions, IN ORDER (kogaki#1385). A Segment's `operation`, `relation` and `manner` shape the Brief Waypoints and are never stated in a Brief Waypoint's `point`. EACH WAYPOINT RECORDS ITS `case` (kogaki#1732): `own` where its paragraph tells the subject's own case, `apart` where it tells a case apart from the subject, in material the reader already holds -- a record of what you decided when you wrote the waypoint, chosen from the Persona's prior knowledge; it adds no paragraph, and the waypoint after an `apart` one carries that case's structure onto the subject, so a Leg never ends on `apart`. THE EXTRACTIONS STAND IN FOR STRANDS (kogaki#1385): paragraph N is the material its Claim stands on, addressed `L<N>` as `claim_strand` in the input; the Leg carries ONE Claim per paragraph. WHAT A PARAGRAPH SAYS AND WHAT IT DOES TO ITS READER ARE READ FROM DIFFERENT PARTS OF THE INPUT (kogaki#1480): Claim N's `proposition` and Brief Waypoint N's `point` are written from paragraph N's `text`, with its Extraction's Segment `content` clauses as that text's sentence-by-sentence reading, and carry no characterisation of the paragraph -- no "exhaustively", "exactly", "only" or "in parallel", nor any other word stating how the paragraph treats its matter rather than what it says; the Extraction's `what_it_does` is never read for a `proposition` or a `point`. The Leg carries exactly as many Brief Waypoints as the original Leg has paragraphs.
REQUIRED RECORD SHAPE: ONE record AT THE RECORD'S OWN ROOT carrying `legs`, an array of EXACTLY ONE Leg, and nothing else. The Leg carries exactly these keys: `leg_id`; `claims` -- one `{ "type": "strand", "strand": "L<N>", "proposition": <one sentence> }` per paragraph, in order; and `waypoints` -- one `{ "point": <one sentence>, "case": "own" | "apart" }` per paragraph, in order. Every field means what src/leg-schema.json, rendered below, says it means. A Leg binds no Move (kogaki#1768): no `move`, `materials`, `purpose`, `rationale`, `depends_on`, `introduces`, `opens_section`, `reaches_target` or `journeys` is composed, and the Leg carries no reader state (kogaki#1699). `leg_ids_already_carried` lists the ids earlier Legs of this Brief carry; this Leg's `leg_id` is none of them.
WHAT IS REFUSED: a key the record or the Leg does not declare, or a declared key missing; not exactly one Leg; a Claim count, or a Brief Waypoint count, other than the paragraph count; Claim N not naming `L<N>`; and the production validators over the Leg, imported: `validateLegs` (its Leg shape, a retired field refused by name, one Claim per Strand, and `waypointsRefusal` over the Brief Waypoints) -- each refused naming the Leg and, where one is at fault, the waypoint. The Section grouping and Reader target rules are whole-path rules about where a Leg sits among others and do not bind a one-Leg Brief (kogaki#1385); and the recoverability criterion (production `recoverabilityOf`, with an empty renderings record): a word of a Claim's `proposition` or a Brief Waypoint's `point` that is in no Segment `content` of the paragraph it serves and is not a function word, refused naming the word and the Segments (kogaki#1495). Each refusal is put back into your next round verbatim.

